    "result_text": "The full build is green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I did\n\nReplaced the membership-level rules with a points system, exposing both `membershipPoints` and `membershipLevel`.\n\n**Points** (start at 0): +2 email present \u00b7 +1 `namesakeCount == 0` \u00b7 +2 household of 3+ \u00b7 +3 tenure over 365 days.\n**Level** maps points: 1 (0\u20131), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+).\n\n## Files touched (4)\n\n- `MembershipLevel.java` \u2014 rewrote into two small pure functions: `points(owner, namesakeCount, householdSize)` and `level(points)` (a one-line `Math.min(points / 2 + 1, 4)`). Reused the existing `hasTenure` helper.\n- `RespondWithOwner.java` / `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java` \u2014 compute points (reusing the existing `Household.size(...)` helper for household size), set both DTO fields. ~3 changed lines each, each in a single-method class (WMC_other = 0).\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` schema field and updated `membershipLevel`'s range/description (drives the generated DTO's `setMembershipPoints`/`setMembershipLevel`).\n\n## Structural-impact notes\n\nThe behavior lives in `MembershipLevel`, a tiny standalone class, so no large class's WMC is dragged in. The two responder edits are trivial and touch single-method classes. The mapping is a closed-form expression rather than a branchy ladder, keeping CC low.\n\nThe acceptance test (`membershipPoints=3`, `membershipLevel=2` for a fresh owner with email, no namesake, single-member household, zero tenure) and the full suite pass \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
